sched/walt: use arch_scale_cpu_capacity() instead of topology_get_cpu_scale()

topology_get_cpu_scale() reads the per-CPU cpu_scale variable, which is
provided by drivers/base/arch_topology.c and exists only on architectures
selecting GENERIC_ARCH_TOPOLOGY (arm, arm64, riscv). On x86 the link fails:

  undefined reference to `cpu_scale'

Use the architecture-neutral arch_scale_cpu_capacity(). On arm/arm64 it
is defined as topology_get_cpu_scale(), so behaviour there is unchanged;
on x86 all clusters get SCHED_CAPACITY_SCALE, which matches symmetric
x86 CPUs.

Affects all architectures without GENERIC_ARCH_TOPOLOGY (e.g. x86).

diff --git a/kernel/sched/walt.c b/kernel/sched/walt.c
index 4391bf669..6cf58794a 100755
--- a/kernel/sched/walt.c
+++ b/kernel/sched/walt.c
@@ -1363,7 +1363,7 @@ static struct sched_cluster *alloc_new_cluster(const struct cpumask *cpus)
 
 	raw_spin_lock_init(&cluster->load_lock);
 	cluster->cpus = *cpus;
-	cluster->efficiency = topology_get_cpu_scale(cpumask_first(cpus));
+	cluster->efficiency = arch_scale_cpu_capacity(cpumask_first(cpus));
 
 	if (cluster->efficiency > max_possible_efficiency)
 		max_possible_efficiency = cluster->efficiency;
